      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 6,
      "title": "Factors Affecting Photosynthesis",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "The rate of photosynthesis isn't always constant. It can speed up or slow down depending on several environmental conditions. These are known as limiting factors, because if any one of them is in short supply, it can limit the overall rate, even if the other factors are abundant. Let's look at the four main factors. First is light intensity. Generally, as light intensity increases, the rate of photosynthesis increases. More light means more energy to drive the reaction. However, this only works up to a certain point. Eventually, the plant's chlorophyll becomes saturated with light, and the rate levels off. Second is carbon dioxide concentration. Just like with light, increasing the concentration of CO₂ in the air will boost the rate of photosynthesis, again, up to a saturation point where other factors become limiting. Third, we have temperature. Photosynthesis relies on enzymes, which are sensitive to temperature. The rate is very low at cold temperatures, increases to an optimal temperature (usually between 25 and 35 degrees Celsius for most plants), and then rapidly decreases if it gets too hot, as the enzymes begin to denature and break down. Finally, water availability is crucial. A shortage of water can cause the stomata on the leaves to close to prevent water loss, which in turn reduces the intake of CO₂, slowing down or even stopping photosynthesis.",
      "narration_segments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "text": "The rate of photosynthesis isn't always constant. It can speed up or slow down depending on several environmental conditions. These are known as limiting factors, because if any one of them is in short supply, it can limit the overall rate, even if the other factors are abundant.",
          "duration": 15
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "text": "Let's look at the four main factors. First is light intensity. Generally, as light intensity increases, the rate of photosynthesis increases. More light means more energy to drive the reaction. However, this only works up to a certain point. Eventually, the plant's chlorophyll becomes saturated with light, and the rate levels off.",
          "duration": 17
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "text": "Second is carbon dioxide concentration. Just like with light, increasing the concentration of CO₂ in the air will boost the rate of photosynthesis, again, up to a saturation point where other factors become limiting.",
          "duration": 12
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "text": "Third, we have temperature. Photosynthesis relies on enzymes, which are sensitive to temperature. The rate is very low at cold temperatures, increases to an optimal temperature (usually between 25 and 35 degrees Celsius for most plants), and then rapidly decreases if it gets too hot, as the enzymes begin to denature and break down.",
          "duration": 19
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "text": "Finally, water availability is crucial. A shortage of water can cause the stomata on the leaves to close to prevent water loss, which in turn reduces the intake of CO₂, slowing down or even stopping photosynthesis.",
          "duration": 12
        }
      ],
